\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}What the comparison contains decides the answer}{10}{subsection.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{res:panels}{{3.2}{10}{What the comparison contains decides the answer}{subsection.3.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces What the comparison contains decides the answer. (a) For each agent, the distance to the nearest incumbent simulant (circles) and to the nearest candidate once the five further compounds treated as simulants in the literature are included (diamonds), as multiples of the typical nearest-neighbour separation of the merged space on the thirty-five-compound panel. A match requires a normalised distance below the dashed threshold \emph  {and} a distance smaller than that to every compound not designated a simulant, so sarin, soman and cyclosarin fail on the primary panel despite lying inside the threshold. (b) Matched agents under 1000 descriptor-resampling replicates (Section~\ref {meth:dboot}) for the incumbent three and for the eight literature-used simulants examined here; circles are means, vertical rules medians, bars the 2.5th to 97.5th percentiles across replicates. (c) On the twenty-eight-compound panel, which omits those five, the distance from each agent to its nearest incumbent simulant in each of the three spaces, normalised by the typical nearest-neighbour separation of that space; filled markers are valid matches. (d) Exact permutation null for that panel over all 680 ways of designating three of the seventeen non-agents as simulants, with the actual designation marked; the $p$ shown is the mean-difference statistic only, the other five and the adjusted values being in Supplementary Table~\ref {S-tab:ablation}, where the Benjamini-Hochberg value over its eighteen tests is 0.165.}}{11}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:main}{{1}{11}{What the comparison contains decides the answer. (a) For each agent, the distance to the nearest incumbent simulant (circles) and to the nearest candidate once the five further compounds treated as simulants in the literature are included (diamonds), as multiples of the typical nearest-neighbour separation of the merged space on the thirty-five-compound panel. A match requires a normalised distance below the dashed threshold \emph {and} a distance smaller than that to every compound not designated a simulant, so sarin, soman and cyclosarin fail on the primary panel despite lying inside the threshold. (b) Matched agents under 1000 descriptor-resampling replicates (Section~\ref {meth:dboot}) for the incumbent three and for the eight literature-used simulants examined here; circles are means, vertical rules medians, bars the 2.5th to 97.5th percentiles across replicates. (c) On the twenty-eight-compound panel, which omits those five, the distance from each agent to its nearest incumbent simulant in each of the three spaces, normalised by the typical nearest-neighbour separation of that space; filled markers are valid matches. (d) Exact permutation null for that panel over all 680 ways of designating three of the seventeen non-agents as simulants, with the actual designation marked; the $p$ shown is the mean-difference statistic only, the other five and the adjusted values being in Supplementary Table~\ref {S-tab:ablation}, where the Benjamini-Hochberg value over its eighteen tests is 0.165}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The spaces the comparison is made in, and what the nominal value is worth once the choices behind it are counted. Red marks the authentic agents, blue the three incumbent simulants and orange the interferents and solvents; only agents and simulants are labelled. (a) Principal components of the structural space and (b) of the three-coordinate GFN2-xTB electronic space, both on the twenty-eight-compound panel, so that the five literature simulants do not appear in them; the leading structural component is a size axis and the leading electronic component loads the two orbital energies against the dipole moment. (c) The mean-difference statistic for 2000 random three-descriptor subspaces of the structural space, before and after ZCA whitening, with the space used here marked: its 0.026 is the 22.4th percentile of those draws, and 27.4\% of them fall below 0.05 as measured, 17\% after whitening. (d) Smallest adjusted value over the eighteen tests of each configuration under the five accounting schemes of Supplementary Table~\ref {S-tab:family}; on the twenty-eight-compound panel only the scheme that fixes the statistic in advance falls below 0.05, and the fourth configuration falls below it under every scheme. Local reactivity at phosphorus and the rank of the Parr index are in Supplementary Figure~\ref {S-fig:plocal}.}}{12}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spaces}{{2}{12}{The spaces the comparison is made in, and what the nominal value is worth once the choices behind it are counted. Red marks the authentic agents, blue the three incumbent simulants and orange the interferents and solvents; only agents and simulants are labelled. (a) Principal components of the structural space and (b) of the three-coordinate GFN2-xTB electronic space, both on the twenty-eight-compound panel, so that the five literature simulants do not appear in them; the leading structural component is a size axis and the leading electronic component loads the two orbital energies against the dipole moment. (c) The mean-difference statistic for 2000 random three-descriptor subspaces of the structural space, before and after ZCA whitening, with the space used here marked: its 0.026 is the 22.4th percentile of those draws, and 27.4\% of them fall below 0.05 as measured, 17\% after whitening. (d) Smallest adjusted value over the eighteen tests of each configuration under the five accounting schemes of Supplementary Table~\ref {S-tab:family}; on the twenty-eight-compound panel only the scheme that fixes the statistic in advance falls below 0.05, and the fourth configuration falls below it under every scheme. Local reactivity at phosphorus and the rank of the Parr index are in Supplementary Figure~\ref {S-fig:plocal}}{figure.2}{}}
